  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nAs of 6z, the satellites are showing some higher clouds moving over \ncentral and northeastern SD and not many signs of fog developing. A \ncouple of models show some patchy fog developing over the James \nRiver Valley along a bit of warm air moving into the area in front \nof a cold front moving in during the morning. However, other models \ndo not show this fog developing. If fog does develop in the\nJames River Valley, it will reduce visibilities slightly, but \nthe visibilities will most likely stay above one mile. The warm \nair moving in overnight and high surface pressure over SD today \nwill help to warm the temperatures to be 10- 20 degrees warmer \nthan normal, even with some cooler air advecting in behind a \ncold front moving through this morning. This cooler air will \nhelp to cool temperatures on Tuesday to be only 10-15 degrees \nwarmer than normal. \n\nTuesday evening into Wednesday, an upper-level shortwave will travel \nalong northwesterly flow aloft over SD. This shortwave will help to \nsupport a surface low pressure that is moving over SD during that \ntime. Some models have shifted the track of the low over SD to the \nsouthwest compared to previous model runs where the low was over ND \nthen moving over MN. This has caused the precipitation chances to be \nshifted to the west. Other models have continued to keep the low \ntrack from ND over northeastern SD into MN, which keeps \nprecipitation chances over far northeastern SD and west central MN \nTuesday evening into Wednesday morning. Because of the variability \nin the models, there is lower confidence in precipitation chances, \nwith a 15-25% chance for scattered rain showers to move over \nportions of north central and northeastern SD Tuesday evening into \nthe early overnight hours. There will not be a lot of rainfall from \nthe rain showers, with a 15-45% chance for 0.01in of rain to fall \nover portions of north central and northeastern SD and a 0-10% \nchance for 0.1 inch of rainfall over far northeastern SD and west \ncentral MN.\n\nBehind the low pressure, the upper-level flow will become northerly \nas some higher surface pressure moves starts to build in Thursday \nand Friday. The northerly flow will push colder air in Wednesday \nthrough Friday, keeping temperatures around normal for this time of \nthe year. Highs will be in the mid 30s to 40s on Thursday because of \nthis. The flow will become southerly during the weekend, which will \nhelp to push warmer than average temperatures back in.",
